$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{x+1}{\sqrt{x+5}-2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x+1}{\sqrt{x+5}-2}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{x+5}+2}{\sqrt{x+5}+2}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the denominator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x+1)(\sqrt{x+5}+2)}{(x+5)-2^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x+1)}(\sqrt{x+5}+2)}{\cancel{(x+1)}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\sqrt{x+5}+2 \text{, for }x\neq -1
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $-1$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
